    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4222010": {
      "title": "Accessibility Services Advisor, Full Time Seasonal",
      "description": "Accessibility Services Advisor, Full Time Seasonal. Niagara University. OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER. Low-cost health benefits, 403b employer contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for you and discounted for eligible immediate family members, generous holidays, and PTO. Campus life is vibrant and offers free parking, on-site dining options, and fresh coffee at Tim Horton's and Starbucks, all in a beautiful park-like setting . If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . *Full Time seasonal 10 month position* JOB SUMMARY: Under general supervision, the Accessibility Services Advisor will assist the Coordinator of Accessibility Services in determining and providing reasonable and appropriate accommodations for registered students with disabilities. The Accessibility Services Advisor will provide academic coaching for incoming first-year students with disabilities and, as needed, with other students who are identified as at-risk. ESSENTIAL DUTIES: Analyze documentation to determine reasonable accommodations for students with disabilities registered with Accessibility Services. Coordinate the provision of accommodations for registered students with disabilities; examples include testing accommodations, notetaking assistance, alternate format of print materials, and disability-related housing requests. Provide information about disability-related services to prospective and current students, parents, faculty, and others. Serve as a liaison between faculty and students with disabilities. Assist the Coordinator of Accessibility Services in developing new and/or updating policies and procedures that address the emerging needs of students with disabilities. Assist the Coordinator of Accessibility Services in completing outcomes assessment work. Provide direct support to students with disabilities as well as some at-risk students participating in the Academic Coaching Program (fall) and the Academic Success Program (spring), assisting them in managing their academic transition to college or improving their academic performance, developing study skills/behaviors, and utilizing a variety of university resources, as needed, to maximize their academic success. Maintain confidentiality of information (including but not limited to: student and employee personal information, student grades, etc.), both during and after employment with the University. Any question regarding the confidentiality of material should be referred to the supervisor. Any violation shall subject the employee to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Perform other duties as assigned. GENERAL DESCRIPTION: % of Time 60% Coordinate the provision of various academic accommodations for registered students with disabilities. 20% Provide academic coaching to first-year students with disabilities and, as needed, to other students who are identified as at-risk; monitor the academic progress of students and maintain appropriate records of the support provided. 10% Determine and develop new and/or updated policies and procedures which address the emerging needs of students with disabilities and assist with the development of written and online information for students with disabilities at Niagara University. 10% Provide information on disability-related support and services to prospective and current students, parents, faculty, and others as requested; serve as a liaison between students with disabilities and faculty, staff, and other university personnel. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Knowledge of disability-related laws and provision of accommodations for students with disabilities. Ability to read and interpret psychological evaluations a s well as other disability-related documentation to determine appropriate accommodations. Experience working with students on the autism spectrum and students with mental health conditions / diagnoses. Knowledge of intervention techniques for at-risk college students, specifically students with disabilities. Excellent oral, written, problem-solving, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively with a diverse population of students, faculty, staff and administrators. Ability to interpret high school transcripts and various student data related to at-risk students. Proficiency with Google Suite. Availability to work nontraditional business hours as needed. ERGONOMIC REQUIREMENTS: The functions of the position are usually performed sitting, but may require some amount of time standing. Some amount of stooping, kneeling, bending, crouching, lifting, walking, carrying, and other movements may be required. Tasks involve working on a computer keyboard and using computer software applications and a calculator that involve extensive wrist and hand movements. All individuals are required to be able to perform these movements without significant risk of injury to themselves or others. QUALIFICATION STANDARDS: Master's Degree in Education, Counseling, Higher Education or related field is required. Two to three years of experience working with students with disabilities in an educational setting or the equivalent is required. A valid New York State driver's license with the ability to travel domestically and internationally (Canada) as required by university operations."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/3800787": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Chemistry Forensic Science (Lecture)",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Chemistry Forensic Science (Lecture). Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . The Department of Chemistry is seeking an adjunct instructor to teach Forensic Science , a course designed for non-science majors with no prior college-level chemistry experience. The course presents scientific and chemical principles within the context of forensic science-the application of science to law. Qualifications: A completed Master's degree in a relevant field (e.g., forensic science, chemistry, or related discipline) is required; a Ph.D. is preferred. College-level teaching preferred; strong content knowledge and interest in teaching also considered. The successful candidate must be able to plan and present instructional material clearly and concisely, apply professional judgment, and maintain positive, supportive relationships with students."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4243932": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - French",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - French. Niagara University. Job Summary: The Department of Modern & Classical Languages has an opening for a part time instructor to teach one section of French 102 – Elementary French II in the Spring 2027 semester. The schedule for this class is Tuesdays/Thursdays 10:30 am – 11:50 am beginning Tuesday, January 19th. Essential Duties: Use a communicative approach to the teaching of a foreign language, using the target language as much as possible, with minimal English for clarification of some grammar points. Prepare daily lesson plans and activities, using a textbook, that engage students in their study of beginning and intermediate French, following a syllabus provided by the Department Chair. Prepare and grade chapter exams and quizzes, with resources provided by the textbook company; manage an online program for daily homework; and evaluate student performance. Manage an online learning management system to post assignments, midterm grades, and final grades. Maintain a class record and keep attendance. Be available for students by appointment to help them with any specific problems they might be having with the course material. Required Knowledge, Skills and Ability: Native or near native French fluency; Thorough knowledge of subject matter; Ability to plan and present instructional material in a clear, logical and concise manner; Ability to maintain proper teacher-student relationship; Sound professional judgment; Initiative and resourcefulness Ability to navigate and manage multiple online platforms for communication and assessment of content Qualifications: Master's Degree in area of specialization preferred. Any questions, please contact Jim McCutcheon, Chair, Department of Modern & Classical Languages, at: jm@niagara.edu"
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/3845245": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Nursing",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Nursing. Niagara University. Adjunct Professor / Graduate Nursing Position Details Position Title Adjunct Faculty, Graduate Nursing Position Status Adjunct Job Description Summary Adjunct Faculty are responsible for teaching advanced practice nursing students in the clinical setting in one of the following specialization areas: Family Nurse Practitioner or Nursing Education. Candidates must demonstrate a commitment to and excellence in graduate teaching. Responsibilities -Serve as a model of professionalism and act as a resource for students within the teaching-learning environment -Maintain required certifications/licensure and demonstrate continuing competence in area of expertise (as applicable) -Participate in all course orientations with Faculty/Program Director as well as orientations related to Canvas, technology, and information resources needed to fulfill teaching responsibilities prior to the start of the class -Initiate and maintain ongoing communication with the Program Director and full-time course faculty in a timely fashion. Provide anecdotal documentation as needed -Comply with all requirements of the College of Nursing for teaching -Comply with all Niagara University expectations for teaching, including posting of syllabi, use of technology, providing students with contact information, responding to students within a reasonable time, being available for communicated office hours with students, following the course grading expectations, and posting grades in accordance with the expectations of the university -Be responsible for competence for classroom/technology use and teaching at the site -Complete self-evaluation of course and review student evaluations of course as a method of ongoing evaluation each semester Minimum Qualifications For Nursing Education: Master's or higher degree in Nursing (degree in Nursing Education preferred) from an accredited institution. Current unencumbered licensure as a registered nurse in New York State. Minimum of two years nursing practice experience required. Teaching experience preferred. Must have flexibility to be available for students. For Family Nurse Practitioner: Board-certified FNP, PNP, ACGNP, ANP, or WHNP. Must be licensed to practice in New York State with a minimum of one year full-time NP practice experience; must have schedule flexibility to be available for students. Preferred Qualifications Prior teaching experience and familiarity with utilization of a learning management system preferred. Applicants should submit a cover letter and resume or curriculum vitae through through https://www.niagara.edu/working-at-niagara/ . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until positions are filled."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/3800783": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Organic Chemistry Laboratory",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Organic Chemistry Laboratory. Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . We are seeking adjunct faculty to teach one or more laboratory sections of Organic Chemistry for Biochemistry and Chemistry majors. Students enrolled in this lab course are also enrolled in a lecture course in organic chemistry. The laboratory component is designed to reinforce and support lecture material through hands-on experiments. All lab experiments are pre-designed and fully developed. Instructors are responsible for facilitating the labs, guiding student learning, and ensuring safe laboratory practices. Qualifications: A completed Master's degree in chemistry or a closely related field is required; a Ph.D. is preferred. College-level teaching preferred; strong content knowledge and interest in teaching also considered. Candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to undergraduate laboratory instruction and adhere to all applicable federal, state, and institutional safety and waste disposal protocols."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/3800777": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Physical Chemistry Laboratory",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Physical Chemistry Laboratory. Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . We are seeking a n adjunct faculty position is available to teach a laboratory section of Physical Chemistry for Biochemistry and Chemistry majors. Students in this course are also enrolled in a lecture course in physical chemistry. The laboratory supports and enhances lecture content through structured, hands-on experimentation. All experiments have been developed and organized in advance. Instructors will be responsible for overseeing the lab sessions, mentoring students in experimental methods and data analysis, and maintaining safety and compliance standards. Qualifications: A completed Master's degree in chemistry or a closely related field is required; a Ph.D. is preferred. College-level teaching preferred; strong content knowledge and interest in teaching also considered. A strong commitment to high-quality undergraduate laboratory instruction and full compliance with safety and waste management practices is essential."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/3343087": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Education – Teacher Education (all subjects)",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor in Education – Teacher Education (all subjects). Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . Adjunct Instructor in Education - Teacher Education (all subjects) The Department of Teacher Education invites applications for part-time adjunct instructor positions for various education courses. Current areas of need are STEM, Literacy and Special Education. In-person and online positions are available. Candidates are expected to have a strong commitment to engaging, interactive, constructivist practices. Candidates must demonstrate commitment to, and excellence in, undergraduate and/or graduate teaching. Applicants should submit a cover letter stating areas of expertise and resume or curriculum vitae through https://www.niagara.edu/working-at-niagara/. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until positions are filled. Required Qualifications: Master of Education degree and minimum of 3 years teaching experience in K-12 settings. Desired Qualifications: Ph.D., ABD, Board Certified, higher education teaching experience, NY state teaching certification, 5+ years of K-12 experience, online teaching experience."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4173867": {
      "title": "Assistant Men’s and Women’s Swimming Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Men’s and Women’s Swimming Coach. Niagara University. OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER. Low-cost health benefits, 403b contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for you and eligible immediate family members, tuition exchange at other designated universities for eligible dependent children, generous holidays (20 days annually), PTO, and ½ day Fridays throughout the summer. Niagara University is also an approved employer for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program. Campus life is vibrant and offers free parking, on-site dining options, and fresh coffee at Tim Horton's and Starbucks, all in a beautiful park-like setting . If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . JOB SUMMARY Under general supervision, the Assistant Swimming Coach assists the head coach in building the swimming program, developing student-athletes academically and athletically, and contributing to the mission of the University. ESSENTIAL DUTIES: The recruitment of student-athletes including athletic and personality assessment, daily correspondence, transcript and video review, logging all activities, on-campus visits, off-campus evaluations, and additional duties as assigned by the head coach. Instructs and mentors student-athletes in the sport of swimming and team building including dryland activities, team activities, weight training, in the water practice and skill development, at competitions, etc. Follows and conforms to NCAA, MAAC, NU athletics department and Niagara University rules and regulations. Maintain confidentiality of information (including but not limited to: student and employee personal information, student grades, etc.), both during and after their employment with the University. Any question regarding the confidentiality of material, should be referred to the supervisor. Any violation shall subject the employee to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Performs other duties as assigned by the head coach including social media, team travel, meals, gear, apparel, parent/family communication, alumni outreach, and additional duties as assigned by the head coach. GENERAL DESCRIPTION: 33% Coaching and developing student-athletes. 33% Recruiting future student-athletes. 33% Operational duties as assigned by the head coach. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Strong interpersonal, organizational, written and oral communication skills. Knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations. Working knowledge of coaching and teaching techniques and the ability to effectively use them. Ability to work evenings, weekends and holidays when necessary. Ability to receive telephone calls, respond to inquiries and communicate with others by telephone. Ability to travel when necessary. Knowledge of recruiting techniques and the ability to effectively use them. Proficiency to handle various office machines including but not limited to: computers, keyboard, software, copy machine, etc. Knowledge of academic eligibility rules. Ability to drive a personal or university vehicle for the purpose of university business. ERGONOMIC REQUIREMENTS The functions of the position usually performed standing, but may require some amount of time sitting, stooping, kneeling, bending, crouching, lifting, walking, running, carrying and other movements. Tasks involving working on a computer keyboard, computer software applications, involve extensive wrist and hand movement. This position requires traveling. All individuals are required to be able to perform these tasks without significant risk of injury to themselves or others. QUALIFICATION STANDARDS Bachelor degree or high school diploma with outstanding high level amateur or professional playing and/or coaching experience required. At least one year of experience coaching swimming at the high school, college or higher competitive level or at least three years experience competing at the college level or higher. A valid driver's license is required that satisfies both University and insurance company underwriting rules and regulations for driving a motor vehicle. Must also be able to operate a motor vehicle in Canada. In good standing with USA Swimming, Safe Sport, and the NCAA."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4174596": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Sport Management",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Sport Management. Niagara University. College of Hospitality, Sport, and Tourism Management Assistant Professor (tenure-track): Sport Management The College of Hospitality, Sport, and Tourism Management at Niagara University is currently accepting applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position to teach in various aspects of sport management to begin no later than August 2027. The College of Hospitality, Sport, and Tourism Management currently enrolls approximately 200 undergraduate and graduate sport management students and sponsors the only graduate program in the College. Preference will be given to applicants that have the ability and expertise to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate level in two or more of these areas: financial management, sport law, and governance. Candidates must provide evidence of strong teaching skills, both in person and through online instruction, and demonstrate a progressive research agenda. It is desirable for the candidate to possess some breadth in their teaching expertise. Sport industry work experience or evidence of collaboration with sport organizations is highly desirable as is the ability to create experiential opportunities and internships for students with sport organizations. Successful applicants will have the ability to shape the curriculum in new ways, taking advantage of the College's unique relationship with industry. The successful candidate will also be expected to support a collegial work environment, engage in scholarly and practical research, advise undergraduate and graduate students, provide service to the University, College, Sport Industry, and community, and to serve as an example of the College's Code of Professionalism. Candidates must hold at least one undergraduate or graduate degree in sport management or business AND a doctoral degree in an applicable discipline from accredited institutions. Applications from individuals at the doctoral degree completion stage are welcomed, however, those applicants will need to show a strong record of industry experience and outreach as well. The college is ACHPA accredited and one of five colleges at Niagara University. It is a nationally ranked program. Founded in 1856, Niagara University is a founding member of Hotel Schools of Distinction and offered the world's first Bachelor's degree in tourism. Niagara University is located in a beautiful tourist setting in Lewiston, New York overlooking Canada and the gorge of the Niagara River, just north of Niagara Falls, USA. Niagara University is committed to providing a student-centered focus via interactive, caring and supportive faculty. To be considered for this position, applications must include: a) cover letter, b) comprehensive curriculum vitae, c) photocopies of all post-secondary academic transcripts, d) a list of at least three professional and academic references . Applicants are encouraged, but not required, to submit teaching evaluations, samples of research, publications or scholarship, letters of recommendation, and any other supporting documents to the applicant system as attachments. Initial review of materials will begin October 1, 2026. This faculty search will remain open until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4192030": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Nursing",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Nursing. Niagara University. Niagara University College of Nursing Faculty Position Position Summary The Niagara University College of Nursing is seeking outstanding nursing faculty candidates for its undergraduate nursing offerings. Faculty positions are full-time and based on Niagara University Lay Teachers Association (NULTA) requirements. The successful candidates will engage in excellence in academic endeavors including laboratory and/or class instruction, clinical expertise, scholarly accomplishments and service based on the National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN) standards. This faculty position will facilitate student learning, identify the various means through which students learn, and support the students' progress through the nursing program, encouraging and cultivating an ethos of learning where mutual respect and responsibility for the student experience is highly regarded. This posting is being advertised pending final budget approval. The University reserves the right to modify or withdraw the posting if funding is not approved. Minimum Qualifications: A current, unencumbered license to practice as a Registered Professional Nurse in New York State A Master's Degree in Nursing (Faculty Fellow), earned Doctorate (Tenure Track ) A demonstrated record of professional and clinical nursing experience, skills laboratory or classroom instruction Preferred Qualifications: At least 5 years' clinical experience as a registered professional nurse At least 2 years' experience of skills lab or classroom instruction, content and syllabi development and the management of student performance At least 2 years' experience of academic nursing instructional experience A doctoral degree in nursing, education, or a related field (Faculty Fellow) Achievement of clinical and /or academic research, presentation and/or publication Duties and Responsibilities Instruction: Fulfills educator role which aligns with the Niagara University College of Nursing model of curricula, student instruction and learning Plans and prepares learning sessions using effective and adaptive strategies to engage students Maintains current knowledge of Niagara University and the College of Nursing academic and attendance policies Actively engage in academic instruction based on selected/assigned courses Student Engagement Supports and engages students to build confidence and competencies in preparing them for a baccalaureate level education Designs classroom experience through the implementation of innovative lesson plans, integration of current content matter which meet the NCSBN standards Establishes and maintains a classroom environment conducive to student learning and development Maintains accurate student records based on assignments, testing modalities and assessment of student knowledge acquisition Submits student grades in a timely manner Professional Faculty Role Participates in processes to provide students with support and guidance in their academic plan through advisement and coaching as appropriate Collaborates with College of Nursing faculty colleagues in the discussion, development, evaluation and implementation of identified courses to promote consistency in instruction and learning Participates in professional development of self and colleagues involving instruction, nursing skills, attendance at University activities, in academic committees and faculty/ staff meetings, curricula development, attendance at conferences, and educational seminars and sessions Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Niagara University is a Catholic University run by the Vincentian order and is located in Western New York on the Niagara River gorge. Regardless of religious affiliation, all faculty members are expected to respect and support the University's mission. The College of Nursing is approved by the New York State Education Department and accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education. To learn more about the College of Nursing visit http://www.niagara.edu ."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4190315": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Residence Life",
      "description": "Associate Director of Residence Life. Niagara University. OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER. Low-cost health benefits, 403b contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for you and eligible immediate family members, tuition exchange at other designated universities for eligible dependent children, generous holidays (20 days annually), PTO, and ½ day Fridays throughout the summer. Niagara University is also an approved employer for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program. Campus life is vibrant and offers free parking, on-site dining options, and fresh coffee at Tim Horton's and Starbucks, all in a beautiful park-like setting . If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . JOB SUMMARY: Under general supervision, the Associate Director of Residence Life is responsible for the management of the residence life program including supervising Residence Director staff, development of living/learning and community standards programs in all residences; recruitment, hiring, and training of all residence hall staff; oversight of programming and community development in all residences; and the resolution of student personnel and management problems in the halls. ESSENTIAL DUTIES: Oversee day-to-day functions of the residence halls, including the staff and students. Supervise and evaluate four residence directors staff members. Hire and train residence directors with the Associate Dean. Assist RDs with the supervision and evaluation of the paraprofessional staff. Co-chair the Students of Concern team, provide follow up and case-management as needed. Liaise with students, family, faculty and administrators to provide support and assistance. Oversee the community development and programming model for the department. Maintain the Student Conduct System online, assign all cases each week, assist in tracking violations, sanctions, and follow up under direction of the Associate Dean or Dean of Students Serve as hearing officer for routine to mid-level violations of the Student Code of Conduct. Meet with at-risk students or those with issues/concerns as assigned by Associate Dean or Dean of Students. Lead in managing student concerns, parent concerns, and work to follow up, address concerns, and connect all with campus resources. Assist in housing operations, including making room changes, billing, room assignments, move in day/move out, break housing, etc. Develop and implement the Community Advisor Selection process each Spring for the following Fall, and as needed throughout the year when vacancies occur. Responsible for the curriculum for paraprofessional training programs for the beginning of each semester, and on an ongoing basis throughout the year, including in-service trainings. Serve in an on-call rotation, maintaining the ability to take phone calls and come to campus if needed during high risk, emergency, and/or crisis situations. Be available as a resource to the Residence Director staff, Campus Safety staff and other University partners. Considered Essential Personnel whose physical presence is required on campus and must also be prepared to address those functions remotely (from off-campus) as needed when operations are restricted or the campus is closed. Support University sustainability initiatives. Develop and maintain programs for sustainability in the residence halls. Serve on University committees as assigned. Review and apply all policies and procedures outlined in the university Affirmative Action Plan. Maintain confidentiality of information (including but not limited to: student and employee personal information, student grades, etc.), both during and after their employment with the University. Any question regarding the confidentiality of material, should be referred to the supervisor. Any violation shall subject the employee to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Collaborate and communicate with university partners on events and programs. Perform all other duties as assigned. Regularly attend and/or facilitate weeknight/weekend programs. GENERAL DESCRIPTION % of time 50% Supervision of staff / day-to-day functions 40% Conduct/Students of Concern 5% Programming 5% Other duties as assigned, supporting other Office of Residence Life/Dining initiatives REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES Ability to work evenings and weekends, to include attendance at student programs; ability to receive telephone calls and e-mail from staff at home. Ability to provide objectivity and patience, to be creative and a team player; to display a commitment to fostering diversity and to demonstrate a commitment to student development. Proficiency to handle various office machines including by not limited to: computer keyboard, computer software applications, calculator, copy machine and fax. Ability to receive telephone calls and e-mails, respond to inquiries and communicate with others by telephone and/or electronically. Strong organizational, interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. Ability to drive a personal or university vehicle in the U.S. and Canada for the purpose of university business. ERGONOMIC REQUIREMENTS: The functions of the position usually performed sitting, but may require some amount of time standing, stooping, kneeling, bending, crouching, lifting, walking, carrying and other movements may be required. Tasks involving working on a computer keyboard, computer software applications, calculator, cutting, labeling, and taping involve extensive wrist and hand movement. All individuals are required to be able to perform these movements without significant risk of injury to themselves or others. QUALIFICATION STANDARDS: Master's degree in Student Personnel, Higher Education Administration or related field is required. P ost-Masters residence life experience / student affairs experience. A valid New York State driver's license is required that satisfies both University and insurance company underwriting rules and regulations for driving a motor vehicle. Must also be able to operate a motor vehicle in Canada."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/1729289": {
      "title": "Clinical Adjunct Position: Clinical Instructor",
      "description": "Clinical Adjunct Position: Clinical Instructor. Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . The College of Nursing at Niagara University seeks part-time Clinical Instructors for pre-licensure Nursing Baccalaureate students in the Traditional 4-year and Accelerated Second Degree Baccalaureate in Science (ASDBS) programs. Clinical rotation sites include area hospitals, healthcare systems, and various healthcare facilities. Clinical experiences are routinely scheduled as one day per week for the 4-year baccalaureate program and two days per week for the ASDBS program. Required: A Master's Degree in Nursing, experience in the specialty area, and an unencumbered current NY State RN license are required. Some teaching experience is preferred. The faculty-student ratio follows NYS Education Department guidelines of a maximum of 1 to 8 faculty/student ratio. Instructor responsibilities include: Planning the clinical experience details with the unit nursing leader to coordinate expectations before the actual start of the student experience. Interacting with clinical agency personnel to maintain effective ongoing communication, to facilitate student learning experiences and positive academic/practice relationships. Preparing student clinical assignments and supervising their experiences in the clinical site. Aligning clinical assignments and experiences with classroom content in collaboration with the Clinical Coordinator as possible. Collaborating regularly with respective theory class faculty members to coordinate course content with clinical experiences. Instructing and supervising students as they provide comprehensive care to patients in the clinical setting. Conducting conferences with students to discuss clinical experiences and the application of theoretical content to patient care. Evaluating students' clinical performance according to course policy using supplied clinical evaluation instrument, in addition to ongoing formative feedback provided to the student. Maintaining attendance records. All applicants are required to be New York State residents."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4156406": {
      "title": "Club Sports Coach- Men's Basketball",
      "description": "Club Sports Coach- Men's Basketball. Niagara University. JOB SUMMARY : Under general supervision, club sport coaches recruit student athletes; assist in the scheduling of games, conducts practices for the assigned team, and contributes to the educational mission of the University by offering direction and instruction in the area of club sports. ESSENTIAL DUTIES : Contributes to the educational mission of the University by coordinating the instruction of student-athletes in the sport that they have been assigned to coach. Recruits student athletes for the respective club sport program. Instructs methods and strategies; coaches student in the sport. Provides motivation, both athletic and academic, to student athletes. If needed, will assistant student leaders in scheduling practices and meets. Supervises assistant coach/coaches. Will assist students in with team travel and at times will be required to supervise players while traveling. Will work with team leaders in providing to provide the Director of the Kiernan Center with update rosters. Coordinate with team leaders the manner in which team scores will be reported to the media for home and away games. Operates the program under the rules and regulations of the university and league rules; understanding that a coach can be suspended without pay or terminated if found to be involved in rule violations. Employees are expected to maintain the confidentiality of all confidential and proprietary information (including but not limited to: student personal information, student grades, employee personal information, etc.), both during and after their employment with the University. If a faculty or staff member has a question about the confidentiality of a particular matter, he/she should consult their supervisor. Any violation of this rule shall subject the faculty /staff member to disciplinary action, up to and including immediate termination of employment. Please refer to your Employee Guide for the University's complete statement on confidentiality. Review and apply all policies and procedures outlined in the university's Affirmative Action Plan. Performs other duties as assigned by the supervisor. GENERAL DESCRIPTION : % of Time 50% Instruct, teach and assist in the development of players during practices; follow and conform to league rules and regulations. 20% Actively coach team during games/scrimmages 15% Recruit players for the following year and tracts make scholarship recommendations. 10% Transports and supervise players when traveling. 5% Provide accurate data to Director of Club Sports about the team and its members. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES : Knowledge of recruiting techniques and the ability to effectively use them. Ability to evaluate potential student athletes during the recruiting process. Knowledge of league rules and regulations. Knowledge of coaching and teaching techniques and the ability to effectively use them; Ability to read and comprehend various forms, documentation, files and information in the department. Strong interpersonal, organizational, written and oral communication skills. Ability to receive telephone calls, respond to inquiries and communicate with others by telephone. Basic mathematical skills including addition, subtraction, multiplication and division to be able to verify accuracy of record keeping and athletics information. Ability to handle routine office operations (typing, filing, record keeping, etc.) Ability to work evenings, weekends and holidays when necessary. Ability to travel when necessary. Ability to drive a personal or university vehicle for the purpose of university business on a regular basis. QUALIFICATION STANDARDS At least one year of experience in coaching at the high school, college or higher competitive level or at least some experience in participating in the respective sport. Valid driver's license required. License must meet insurance company rules and regulations for driving underwriting criteria. Must take and pass the \"American Red Cross CPR/AED for the Professional Rescuer.\" ERGONOMIC REQUIREMENTS : The functions of the position usually performed standing, but may require some amount of time sitting. Some amount of stooping, kneeling, bending, crouching, lifting, walking, carrying and other movements may be required. Tasks involving instruction in fitness activities involve running, walking and lifting. Tasks involve working on a computer keyboard, typewriter, calculator, cutting, labeling, and taping involve involves extensive wrist and hand movements. All individuals are required to be able to perform these movements without significant risk of injury to themselves or others. NOTE : MANAGEMENT RESERVES THE RIGHT TO CHANGE OR REASSIGN JOB DUTIES OR COMBINE POSITIONS AT ANY TIME."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4210315": {
      "title": "Director of the Office of Violence Prevention & Education",
      "description": "Director of the Office of Violence Prevention & Education. Niagara University. DIRECTOR, OFFICE OF VIOLENCE PREVENTION & EDUCATION Niagara University | Student Affairs | Full-Time, Exempt OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER Low-cost health benefits, 403(b) employer contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for employees and eligible immediate family members, tuition exchange at other designated universities for eligible dependent children, generous holidays (20 days annually), PTO, and half-day Fridays throughout the summer. Niagara University is also an approved employer for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program. Campus life offers free parking, on-site dining, and a beautiful park-like setting overlooking the Niagara River gorge. ABOUT NIAGARA UNIVERSITY Niagara University is a liberal arts university steeped in the Catholic and Vincentian traditions, with nearly 4,300 students. Located a few miles from Niagara Falls, New York, the University offers a close-knit, mission-driven community committed to academic excellence, service, and the dignity of every person. POSITION SUMMARY Niagara University seeks an experienced, collaborative, and forward-thinking Director to lead the Office of Violence Prevention & Education (OVPE). The Director will shape and coordinate the University's comprehensive efforts to prevent domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, stalking (DVDVSAS), and related forms of gender-based violence. The position oversees OVPE staff, operations, programming, communications, assessment, budget, and applicable U.S. Department of Justice, Office on Violence Against Women (OVW) Campus Program grant activities. The Director works across the University and with community partners to advance evidence-informed, trauma-informed, culturally responsive, accessible, and coordinated prevention and response practices. As a Student Affairs leader, the Director also serves in the rotating Administrator On-Call schedule for critical student situations after hours and on weekends. ESSENTIAL DUTIES Provide strategic and operational leadership for OVPE and serve as the University's central coordinator for DVDVSAS prevention and education. Supervise and develop professional and student staff; establish priorities, work plans, and performance expectations; and manage the office budget and resources. Design, deliver, and assess a comprehensive prevention and education strategy for students, faculty, and staff, including bystander intervention, consent, healthy relationships, social-norms change, resource awareness, and population-specific initiatives. Lead the campus Coordinated Community Response Team (CCRT), serve on the Niagara County Sexual Assault Response Team (SART), and build strong relationships with victim-service providers, law enforcement, health professionals, and other community partners. Administer related grant activities, including strategic planning, required training and travel, records, budgets, data collection, reporting, and coordination with technical-assistance providers and University offices. Collaborate with OVPE Faculty Fellows, the Office for Title IX and Civil Rights, Campus Safety, Dean of Students, Residential Living, Health and Counseling Services, faculty researchers, and other partners to strengthen prevention, referrals, training, and coordinated response while maintaining appropriate role clarity and reporting obligations. Develop engaging campaigns, workshops, mandatory and ongoing education, digital resources, and communications; maintain the OVPE website and programming calendar. Use data, learning outcomes, program evaluation, and current research to measure effectiveness and continuously improve programs and institutional practices. Serve on the Students of Concern team and other University committees or initiatives as assigned. Participate in the rotating Administrator On-Call schedule, including evenings, overnight hours, weekends, holidays, and academic breaks; provide timely consultation, coordinate notifications and follow-up, and report to campus when circumstances require. Maintain discretion and confidentiality, fulfill applicable reporting and documentation requirements, and perform other duties consistent with the role. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in higher education, public health, counseling, social work, psychology, criminology/criminal justice, education, or a related field. Three to five years of progressively responsible experience in gender-based violence prevention, prevention education, victim services, public health, student affairs, or a related field. Demonstrated knowledge of primary prevention, trauma-informed and survivor-centered practice, coordinated community response, culturally responsive programming, and accessibility. Experience leading programs or projects, supervising staff or student employees, and collaborating effectively across organizational and community partners. Experience with program assessment and with budget and/or grant administration. Excellent written, oral, facilitation, presentation, and relationship-building skills. Ability to respond to sensitive and critical situations with sound judgment, calm, discretion, and appropriate urgency. Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends, travel as needed, participate in the Administrator On-Call rotation, and report to campus after hours when required. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Higher education experience. Experience administering an OVW Campus Program grant or a comparable federal or state grant. Experience leading a CCRT, SART, or comparable multidisciplinary partnership. Working knowledge of the Clery Act/VAWA, Title IX, New York Education Law Article 129-B, and related higher education requirements. Experience with campus-wide campaigns, bystander-intervention programs, mandatory education, and program evaluation. POSITION DETAILS Employment Status Regular full-time; exempt; administrative Work Location Gallagher Center, Niagara University, New York Reporting To Vice President for Student Affairs Compensation $ 58,000 - $ 65,000 annually, commensurate with qualifications and experience Anticipated Start September, 2026 APPLICATION PROCESS To apply, please submit a cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references through Niagara University's online employment portal. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. EQUAL OPPORTUNITY AND ACCOMMODATIONS Niagara University affirms its commitment to equal opportunity and non-discrimination and recognizes its responsibility to provide all employees an environment free of discrimination and harassment based on sex, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, race, color, religion, national origin, age, marital status, familial status, domestic violence victim status, pregnancy-related condition, ex-offender status, military status, veteran status, disability, or any other category protected by law. The University also prohibits actual or attempted reprisal or retaliation against an employee who raises a sincere and valid concern regarding harassment or discrimination. Individuals who need reasonable accommodations under the ADA to participate in the search process should contact the Niagara University Human Resources Office at 716.286.8690."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4181899": {
      "title": "Faculty Fellow, Department of Education in Ontario",
      "description": "Faculty Fellow, Department of Education in Ontario. Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, be sure to check out all of the NU Facts . Niagara University is seeking a full-time Faculty Fellow in the Department of Education for the campus in Vaughan, Ontario for the Spring of 2027. The successful candidate will teach the equivalent of four courses per semester and support the efforts of the college in service to the profession. Applicants should hold a master's degree and OCT certification. The successful candidate will have at least 3 years of experience teaching in P-12 schools. Special preference will be given to candidates with experience teaching in higher education, and experience teaching students with diverse learning needs."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/1795063": {
      "title": "Part-Time Adjunct Instructor - Department of Mathematics",
      "description": "Part-Time Adjunct Instructor - Department of Mathematics. Niagara University. Niagara University is a liberal arts university, steeped in the Vincentian and Catholic traditions, with a total enrollment of nearly 4,300 students. Located just a few miles away from one of the world's premier tourism destinations, Niagara Falls, the university occupies a lovely campus overlooking the Niagara River gorge. Its close proximity to major cultural and business centers in Niagara Falls, Buffalo, and Toronto as well as the award-winning Niagara Wine Trail and several nearby cultural destinations make Niagara University an ideal place to work and live. If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . The Department of Mathematics at Niagara University invites applications for a part-time adjunct instructor position on a need basis starting in fall 2025. A master's degree, preferably in mathematics, is required. Candidates must demonstrate commitment to, and excellence in, undergraduate teaching. Part-time instructors will teach one to two classes per semester. Course assignments may include introductory statistics, precalculus, Calculus I, math for the liberal arts, or introduction to college mathematics. Applicants should submit a cover letter and resume or curriculum vitae through https://www.niagara.edu/working-at-niagara/ Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to the Department Chairperson, Dr. Joel Louwsma, at jlouwsma@niagara.edu . Niagara University was founded in 1856 by the Catholic Vincentian Fathers, whose mission emphasizes practical training and excellence in the service of society. NU's traditional stone buildings and leafy green campus sit on Monteagle Ridge, overlooking the Niagara River Gorge just four miles north of the majestic Niagara Falls. The university is 30 minutes from Buffalo, which offers a variety of cultural events, sports, and entertainment opportunities, and 90 minutes from Toronto, Canada's largest metropolitan area. Niagara University is an equal opportunity/affirmative action, employer. Consistent with our Catholic mission, Niagara University is committed to providing employment and educational opportunities and will not discriminate on the basis of race, age, sex (gender), national origin, religious preference, sexual orientation, predisposing genetic characteristic, or status as veteran or disability. Women and minorities are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4243687": {
      "title": "Student Accounts Operations Manager",
      "description": "Student Accounts Operations Manager. Niagara University. OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER. Low-cost health benefits, 403b employer contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for you and eligible immediate family members, tuition exchange at other designated universities for eligible dependent children, generous holidays (20 days annually), PTO, and ½ day Fridays throughout the summer. Niagara University is also an approved employer for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program. Campus life is vibrant and offers free parking, on-site dining options, and fresh coffee at Tim Horton's and Starbucks, all in a beautiful park-like setting . If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . JOB SUMMARY : Under general supervision, the Student Accounts Operations Manager assists in the supervision of work flow in the Student Accounts Office. Responsibilities include but are not limited to managing the daily billing process; disbursement of financial aid; issuing student refunds; updates to the GL process; monitoring the out of school payment plan module; working with other university departments, students and families to resolve financial aid disbursement and billing concerns. The position assumes the responsibility for the Student Accounts operation in the absence of the Director and supervises two staff members. ESSENTIAL DUTIES : Assist the Director in the billing process and collection of student receivables Work closely with the Director to provide leadership to the department and supervise two staff members who report directly to the position Provide direct leadership for counseling students and families on financing/payment options, holds, billing, collections and compliance Routinely updates billing tables, writes and troubleshoots billing rules in the Colleague student information system Manage and serve as the primary point person for the Bill/Payment module of the TouchNet billing system Disburses financial aid to student accounts Processes meal plan requests for student athletes and commuters Monitor refund reports to ensure accuracy; issue refunds to students within mandated timeframes Monitor outstanding check list and follow up on uncashed refund checks Review tuition accounts for pre-collection, collection and write off Work with delinquent payers, creating payment plans as needed; manage the out of school payment plan module Assist in filing annual 1098T, 1042S, T2202, T4A and TL11 tax forms Present to students and parents at new student orientation Provide coverage for front line customer service team and cashiering as needed Works closely with Financial Aid, the Registrar, the Controller's Office, and other university departments; ensures that functions performed are in compliance with federal and state regulations and University policies and procedures Review and apply all policies and procedures outlined in the university Affirmative Action Plan Maintain confidentiality of information (including but not limited to: student and employee personal information, student grades, etc.), both during and after their employment with the University. Any question regarding the confidentiality of material, should be referred to the supervisor. Any violation shall subject the employee to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Perform other duties and projects as assigned GENERAL DESCRIPTION : % of Time 35% Run billing process, maintain billing tables, disburse financial aid 30% Review and voucher student refunds; follow up on uncashed refund checks 20% Monitor the out of school payment plan and tuition collection processes 10% Counsel students, parents, faculty and staff on billing policies 5% Supervise staff members that report directly to the position REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES : Strong interpersonal customer service skills and written communication skills necessary to interact with students, parents and outside agencies. Experience interpreting and explaining complex policies and processes to customers in sometimes stressful situations Knowledge of basic math skills, basic accounting to verify tuition and loan payments and collection activities. Experience working in Colleague, TouchNet or similar information systems. Ability to receive telephone calls, respond to inquiries and communicate with others by telephone, email or other virtual means. Knowledge of financial operations with strong independent judgment skills. Ability to work on a project from start to finish with little or no help from the supervisor. Ability to interpret instructions and reports given by the controller and the functional officer. QUALIFICATION STANDARDS: Bachelor's degree is required A minimum of 3 years experience in student accounts, student financial services, accounts receivable, accounting or a related field A minimum of 2 years of managerial experience is preferred Knowledge of federal and state regulations; organizational skills and ability to effectively communicate with students and parents in a high-pressure environment"
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4143395": {
      "title": "Temporary Employment - Assistant Dance Line Coach",
      "description": "Temporary Employment - Assistant Dance Line Coach. Niagara University. JOB SUMMARY: To help Head Coach by attending some practices and both Men's and some Women's Basketball games, as well as providing feedback to the team when needed. Helping with or providing choreography to the team at times. ESSENTIAL DUTIES: Lead practices when necessary or provide feedback on team based choreography and help sharpen routines. Work closely with Head Coach on a practice & game schedule for the team, up to and during basketball season. Knowledge of basic audio equipment to for practices Maintain confidentiality of information (including but not limited to: student and employee personal information, student grades, etc.), both during and after their employment with the University. Any question regarding the confidentiality of material, should be referred to the supervisor. Any violation shall subject the employee to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Perform other duties as assigned. GENERAL DESCRIPTION: 50% Practice and Game attendance. 25% Choreography 25% Working with Head Coach on ideas for team for fundraising, community service, costumes for competitions and game days. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES Dance experience Teaching dance experience a plus Work well with the traditional college-age student population Ability to work non-traditional business hours including weekends & evenings QUALIFICATION STANDARDS include a Bachelor's degree; a minimum of one year experience in related field A valid New York State driver's license is required that satisfies both University and insurance company underwriting rules and regulations for driving a motor vehicle"
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4197050": {
      "title": "Title IX Coordinator and Civil Rights Officer",
      "description": "Title IX Coordinator and Civil Rights Officer. Niagara University. OUTSTANDING BENEFITS UNLIKE ANY OTHER. Low-cost health benefits, 403b employer contribution, affordable tuition at Niagara University for you and eligible immediate family members, tuition exchange at other designated universities for eligible dependent children, generous holidays (20 days annually), PTO, and ½ day Fridays throughout the summer. Niagara University is also an approved employer for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program. Campus life is vibrant and offers free parking, on-site dining options, and fresh coffee at Tim Horton's and Starbucks, all in a beautiful park-like setting . If you'd like to know more, check out all of the NU Facts . NIAGARA UNIVERSITY TITLE IX COORDINATOR AND CIVIL RIGHTS OFFICER Position Posting Status Regular Full-Time; Exempt Department Office for Title IX and Civil Rights Sector Student Affairs Reports To Vice President for Student Affairs Location O'Shea Hall, Lewiston, New York Salary Range $80,000-$85,000 annually Anticipated Start As soon as practicable (Fall 2026) ABOUT NIAGARA UNIVERSITY Niagara University is a Catholic and Vincentian institution of higher education committed to academic excellence, service, and the dignity of every person. The University seeks a thoughtful, experienced, and collaborative professional to lead its institution-wide Title IX and civil rights compliance work and to help sustain fair, accessible, and responsive processes for students, faculty, staff, applicants, and other members of the University community. THE OPPORTUNITY The Title IX Coordinator and Civil Rights Officer provides institution-wide leadership for Niagara University's compliance with Title IX and related federal and state sex-discrimination requirements and is intended to serve as the University's designated Title VI Coordinator pursuant to New York Education Law. The position leads the Office for Title IX and Civil Rights and coordinates the University's response to reports of discrimination, harassment, sexual misconduct, and retaliation. The Coordinator works closely with Human Resources and General Counsel on employment matters and partners broadly across Student Affairs, Academic Affairs, Campus Safety, Athletics, the Office of Violence Prevention & Education, and other University and community resources. PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES Provide central leadership and coordination for Title IX compliance and, if designated, serve as Niagara University's Title VI Coordinator. Oversee intake, outreach, supportive measures, case assessment, grievance and resolution processes, and appropriate role separation among investigators, decision-makers, appeal officers, and other process participants. Lead the review, implementation, and periodic revision of Title IX, Title VI, sexual misconduct, non-discrimination, grievance, and related policies and procedures. Coordinate the institutional response to discrimination, harassment, and retaliation concerns and work closely with Human Resources and General Counsel on employee and Title VII matters. Collaborate with the office of Violence Prevention & Education on required and role-specific training for students, faculty, staff, administrators, and Title IX/civil rights personnel. Maintain legally compliant records and case-management practices; monitor timelines, outcomes, trends, and systemic concerns; and use data to strengthen institutional practice. Coordinate with Campus Safety, Student Affairs, Human Resources, OVPE, and other partners on Clery, VAWA, and New York Education Law requirements. Serve as a key liaison with government and regulatory agencies in consultation with General Counsel and appropriate University leadership. Build effective relationships across the University and with community partners while maintaining clear distinctions among compliance, investigation, adjudication, advocacy, prevention, clinical, and law-enforcement functions. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in higher education, law, public administration, human resources, student affairs, social sciences, counseling/social work, organizational leadership, or a closely related field. Minimum of three years of progressively responsible professional experience in higher education compliance, civil rights/equal opportunity, legal affairs, human resources, student affairs, investigations, or a related field, including substantial direct responsibility for Title IX or comparable civil rights case management and compliance work. Demonstrated experience applying Title IX regulations and institutional non-discrimination or sexual misconduct policies, including intake, supportive measures, investigations or investigation oversight, resolution-process coordination, and required documentation. Demonstrated experience developing or revising policy, delivering training, managing sensitive records and data, and collaborating across institutional functions on complex compliance matters. Ability to satisfy the schedule, availability, professional-development, and response expectations of an institution-wide compliance leadership role. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior service as a Title IX Coordinator, Deputy Title IX Coordinator, Title VI Coordinator, civil rights/equal opportunity officer, or comparable higher education compliance leader. Current or recent Title IX Coordinator, civil rights investigator, or related certification/training through ATIXA or another recognized higher education compliance organization. Experience administering Title VI compliance and New York higher education requirements, including Education Law § 6436-a and Article 129-B. Experience coordinating institutional responses to OCR, EEOC, or state civil rights agency matters and working at the intersection of Title IX, Title VI, Title VII, Clery/VAWA, student conduct, and employment processes. THE SUCCESSFUL CANDIDATE The successful candidate will bring strong knowledge of higher education civil rights law and practice; sound judgment, neutrality, discretion, and empathy; excellent policy, investigative, training, and communication skills; and the ability to lead complex compliance work across organizational lines. The role requires an individual who can respond to sensitive matters with care while maintaining the impartiality, procedural rigor, documentation, and institutional perspective essential to an effective Title IX and civil rights program. COMPENSATION The anticipated salary range for this full-time, exempt position is $80,000-$85,000 annually, commensurate with qualifications and experience and subject to University compensation practices. APPLICATION Interested candidates should apply through Niagara University's employment process and submit the materials requested in the University's online application. Niagara University encourages applications from candidates who can demonstrate both substantive compliance expertise and a strong commitment to collaborative, mission-centered higher education practice."
    },
    "https://niagara.applicantpro.com/jobs/4253383": {
      "title": "Visiting Instructor - Biology",
      "description": "Visiting Instructor - Biology. Niagara University. Job Description- Faculty - Visiting Laboratory Instructor POSITION SUMMARY The Niagara University College of Arts and Sciences Department of Biology is seeking outstanding Visiting Laboratory Microbiology Instructor candidates. The Laboratory Instructor will be responsible for teaching microbiology with laboratory courses in the Department of Biology for both biology and nursing majors. We seek applicants with an interest in fostering rigorous, inclusive, and high-impact learning environments. Candidates of diverse backgrounds are encouraged to apply. The position will be Spring Semester 2027, from January until May. Submit cover letter, C.V., and three references along with a statement of teaching philosophy through the application portal. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A Master's degree or higher in Medical Microbiology and/or Biomedical sciences or related field. Demonstrated experience in microbiological techniques. Teaching experience at the university level. Candidates should demonstrate knowledge of and the ability to use technology to support instruction. An understanding and commitment to working with faculty, staff, and students from diverse cultures and backgrounds. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Doctorate in Medical Microbiology, Biomedical sciences and/or a related field. Demonstrated ability to teach core courses in biology (e.g., microbiology, anatomy and physiology, introductory biology or capstone writing course). In addition to a strong academic background, applicants with experience supporting research training for undergraduates are encouraged to apply."
    }
  },
